The above argument does not extend to any $n \times n$ or squarish lower part of an $n$-gon, since that would cycle through the same arguments. Instead, we develop a logic that can extend for larger $n$-gons. So if we list all the interior angles (i.e, angle 1, angle 2, etc…), we get that the sum of all the interior angles in an $n$-gon is $(n-2)\times 180$. If the $n$-gon only has right angles, the sum of its interior angles would be $90n$. And when the sum of the interior angles of an $n$-gon is less than $90n$, the polygon is not able to have any right angles. Therefore, for an $n$-gon to have as many right angles as allowed, the sum of its interior angles must be at least $(n-2)\times 180$.

